WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.122
Cloud network security is about controlling what is reachable, from where, and for what purpose.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:15.630
A cloud service may be private, internal to a virtual network, exposed through a controlled entry point, or reachable from the public internet.

3
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.240
Public exposure is not automatically wrong. Many applications need public endpoints.

4
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.666
The security question is whether that exposure is intentional, minimal, protected, and monitored.

5
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:37.215
Cloud networks are built from several control layers. Security groups, firewall rules, routing tables, load balancers, private endpoints, network access controls, and service-level settings all influence reachability.

6
00:00:37.335 --> 00:00:43.457
A resource can look private at one layer and still be reachable through another path.

7
00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:50.516
That is why network review should focus on effective exposure, not just one setting in one place.

8
00:00:50.636 --> 00:00:59.616
Segmentation limits unnecessary communication. A public web tier might need to receive traffic from users and connect to a private application tier.

9
00:00:59.736 --> 00:01:11.164
The application tier might need limited access to a database. The database usually should not accept connections from the internet, from every workload, or from every team network.

10
00:01:11.284 --> 00:01:16.182
Segmentation helps contain mistakes and makes normal communication easier to reason about.

11
00:01:16.302 --> 00:01:24.057
Good firewall and security group rules are specific. They avoid broad source ranges unless there is a documented need.

12
00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:30.300
They limit ports to what the service actually uses. They name ownership and purpose clearly.

13
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.950
They are reviewed when applications change. Temporary access rules should expire or be removed after use.

14
00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:44.009
A rule that was safe during a migration can become risky if it is forgotten for months.

15
00:01:44.129 --> 00:01:50.660
Network controls also connect to identity and data protection. A private endpoint does not replace IAM.

16
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:58.535
A restrictive firewall rule does not replace encryption. A public endpoint does not have to mean weak access control.

17
00:01:58.655 --> 00:02:07.226
These controls work together. The safest design usually combines intentional exposure, strong identity, encrypted transport, service-level authorization, logging, and alerting.

18
00:02:07.346 --> 00:02:12.652
A practical network review asks four questions. What is reachable from the internet?

19
00:02:12.772 --> 00:02:18.895
What can talk to what inside the cloud environment? Which ports and protocols are open?

20
00:02:19.015 --> 00:02:23.913
Which services should be private or reachable only through controlled entry points?

21
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.931
Teams should document the expected answers and compare them against actual configuration.

22
00:02:29.051 --> 00:02:39.663
When the actual network does not match the intended architecture, the next step is safe remediation: tighten rules, add segmentation, update owners, and monitor for recurrence.
